    "summary": "Valley Road shines, Bird Song charms, but a jam cut mars flow",
    "blurb": "Valley Road dominates both sets, while Victim Or The Crime and Standing On The Moon provide strong vocal moments. Jerry’s vocals are praised on Desolation Row and the titular moon song, and Vince’s keyboard mix settles as the night progresses. A missing segment of the Bird Song jam slightly disrupts the otherwise fresh set flow.",
    "review": "On October 22 1990 at Frankfurt’s Festhalle the Dead delivered a lively European outing that feels both fresh and oddly uneven. The first set opens with a sluggish Bertha but quickly recovers as Greatest Story Ever Told, Peggy‑O and a spirited Foolish Heart launch into a standout Desolation Row – Jerry’s vocals were described as “standing on the moon” and right on the money. Valley Road caps the first half with a soaring performance that reviewers across recordings flag as a highlight. The second set erupts with Victim Or The Crime, Standing On The Moon and a tight Playing In The Band into Uncle John’s Band, leading to an intense drum‑and‑space excursion before a heartfelt All Along The Watchtower, Stella Blue and Turn On Your Lovelight. Bird Song offers a beautiful moment, though an abrupt cut in the jam (around 7:39) frustrates listeners. Overall the show is solid with several standout moments, but the missing jam fragment keeps it from reaching classic status.\n\nFor fans seeking the best listening experience the clear matrix mix (gd90-10-22.sbd.gardner.8722.sbeok.shnf) captures the band’s energy and balances instruments well, only blemished by the Bird Song cut. Those who prefer pristine board sound may opt for the digital SBD (gd90-10-22.dsbd.hinko.22315.sbeok.shnf), which presents excellent clarity and a slightly high‑early Vince mix that settles later in the night.",
